      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Let's denote the number of bacteria at the end of day \\( n \\) as \\( B(n) \\). According to the problem, the number of bacteria doubles every day, and the colony starts with 3 bacteria. Therefore, we can express the number of bacteria at the end of day \\( n \\) as:\n\\[ B(n) = 3 \\cdot 2^n \\]\n\nWe need to find the smallest \\( n \\) such that \\( B(n) > 100 \\). This means we need to solve the inequality:\n\\[ 3 \\cdot 2^n > 100 \\]\n\nFirst, we divide both sides of the inequality by 3:\n\\[ 2^n > \\frac{100}{3} \\]\n\\[ 2^n > 33.33 \\]\n\nNext, we need to find the value of \\( n \\) that satisfies this inequality. We can do this by evaluating successive powers of 2:\n\\[ 2^5 = 32 \\]\n\\[ 2^6 = 64 \\]\n\nSince \\( 2^5 = 32 \\) is less than 33.33 and \\( 2^6 = 64 \\) is greater than 33.33, we conclude that \\( n = 6 \\) is the smallest integer that satisfies the inequality.\n\nTo verify, we calculate \\( B(6) \\):\n\\[ B(6) = 3 \\cdot 2^6 = 3 \\cdot 64 = 192 \\]\n\nSince 192 is indeed greater than 100, our solution is correct. Therefore, the number of the first day which ends with the colony having more than 100 bacteria is:\n\\[ \\boxed{6} \\]",
